comments.body:
One of the most annoying thing in the React InstantSearch codebase is to conditionally support single and multi index states. I thought we were going to a different structure in InstantSearch.js that will make development easier: always have the index ID as key and remove all top-level states.

```ts
interface IndexUiState {
  query?: string;
  // ...
}

interface UiState {
  [indexId: string]: IndexUiState;
}
```

An example would be:

```ts
const state: UiState = {
  movies: {
    query: 'hammer',
  },
  songs: {
    query: 'burning',
  },
};
```

The role of state mapping would be to transform any single-index mapping to a `IndexUiState` to keep routing backward compatible.

> Before this change, UiState was merging the indexIds directly with the other state,

Not sure to understand the meaning of the comment. Could you explain? The current format of `uiState` is already the one described by @francoischalifour. I don't think we have to update its structure. The types inside the codebase are not up-to-date though, this is the idea:

```ts
type UiState = {
  [indexId: string]: IndexUiState;
};

const movies: IndexUiState = {
  query: "hammer"
};

const songs: IndexUiState = {
  query: "burning"
};

const uiState: UiState = {
  songs,
  movies
};
```
